      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/gesposito/mobile-agent",
      "date": "2026-10-30",
      "authors": [
        "gesposito"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how maintaining a live mirror of mobile screen state eliminates round-trip latency during AI agent interaction.",
      "hn_id": "50310027",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50310027",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most mobile agent frameworks fail because the observation loop is too slow. Issuing a screenshot or dumping an accessibility tree over adb introduces hundreds of milliseconds of latency per action, forcing agents to sleep arbitrarily between taps.\n\nMobile-agent tackles this latency problem by maintaining an in-memory, live mirror of every attached device screen. Instead of querying the operating system synchronously on every step, a background daemon subscribes directly to platform frame callbacks and accessibility change events. The agent answers snapshot, wait, and settle requests immediately from local state.\n\nOn Android, the project pushes a single-dex agent directly under app_process with shell privileges, avoiding APK installation overhead entirely. Exposing this interface over the Model Context Protocol (MCP) gives LLM agents real-time inspection capabilities with minimal overhead.\n\nOptimizing the observation pipeline transforms mobile agents from brittle screen-scrapers into responsive automation tools."
    },
    {
