      "content": "Telling an autonomous coding agent to remember its past mistakes in a system prompt or documentation file does not work. When running Claude Code daily across production repositories, teams discovered that agent memory across sessions decays quickly, causing identical destructive errors to recur indefinitely.\n\nPrompting cannot fix structural reliability issues because soft guidance fails at execution boundaries. Instead of adding more natural language warnings, the better pattern is introducing mechanical enforcement gates via pre-execution hooks and pre-ship checks that physically intercept commands.\n\nThis open-source collection provides seven deterministic Python hooks built from a real dataset of over 500 logged agent failures. Each guard runs locally with standard library dependencies and features regression tests verifying that illegal file deletions, premature git pushes, and broken shell executions are caught before execution.\n\nHard boundary checks beat soft prompt engineering every single time when building production agent harnesses."
