    {
      "title": "Profiling disk wait time directly with eBPF flame graphs",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://coroot.com/blog/disk-io-profiling-with-ebpf/",
      "date": "2026-10-30",
      "authors": [
        "Nikolay Sivko"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn why generic off-CPU profiling is too noisy for diagnosing storage bottlenecks and how targeted eBPF tracing attributes disk latency directly to application stack traces.",
      "hn_id": "50306721",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50306721",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Standard CPU flame graphs leave engineers completely blind when a database stalls on disk I/O. Because waiting on block storage does not consume CPU cycles, the CPU profile stays flat even as volume latency spikes and query queues back up.\n\nOff-CPU profiling attempts to solve this by hooking the operating system scheduler whenever a thread yields execution. In practice, this technique fails for databases. A database engine running hundreds of connection threads spends the majority of its off-CPU time sleeping on idle network sockets, timers, or mutexes. The real disk wait signals get buried in thousands of irrelevant idle thread stacks.\n\nCoroot solved this bottleneck by using targeted eBPF tracepoints attached directly to the block layer and asynchronous I/O engines like io_uring and libaio. Instead of tracking every thread context switch, the probe captures the precise application call stack only when an actual storage request begins and completes.\n\nThis connects slow queries directly to the internal database functions initiating the read or write operation without adding overhead.\n\nObservability should pinpoint root causes in your code rather than just reporting disk utilization."
    },
    {
      "title": "Fiber-based job systems give programmers greater control over scheduling",
      "source": "hn",
