      "content": "Preemptive OS threads introduce substantial context switching overhead and non-deterministic scheduling latency. In high-throughput graphics engines and simulation runtimes, relying purely on the operating system scheduler can easily throttle performance and create contention across CPU cores.\n\nBuilding a fiber-based job system replaces kernel preemption with cooperative user-space scheduling. Because fibers switch execution state explicitly at known synchronization points, the engine avoids unneeded stack allocations and expensive kernel transitions. The scheduler can pack execution context tightly and distribute tasks evenly across dedicated worker threads aligned with physical cores.\n\nThis architecture eliminates lock convoys and minimizes cache thrashing under heavy workloads. Designing your runtime around cooperative fibers turns concurrency from an OS-level gamble into a deterministic, fine-grained pipeline."
    },
    {
