      "content": "Language models can reliably detect, pinpoint, and classify non-semantic disruptions injected directly into their intermediate activations. Recent experiments across model architectures from 8B to 36B parameters reveal that transformers can identify whether their representations were altered by dropout or Gaussian noise.\n\nThis behavior is not explained by perplexity spikes or simple shifts in output token likelihood. Models like Qwen and Olmo demonstrate zero-shot localization of internal layer perturbations, with detection accuracy scaling alongside perturbation magnitude. Even more notable, models struggle when supervised with incorrect labels during context steering, revealing an intrinsic bias toward true activation states.\n\nThese findings suggest that transformers maintain internal representations capable of monitoring their own computational trajectories. As we build autonomous agent frameworks and evaluate output reliability, introspecting activation degradation could provide an architectural path toward automated hallucination detection and latent error trapping."
    },
    {
