      "content": "Sandboxing untrusted workloads typically forces a hard compromise. You either spin up a heavyweight virtual machine for isolation, or you run a lightweight container that lacks a complete init system and exposes your host kernel.\n\ngVisor changes this equation by adding support for systemd inside its sandbox runtime. By implementing core kernel primitives such as cgroups v2, pidfds, the new mount API, and POSIX ACLs, gVisor can now boot full system images like Ubuntu or Debian without requiring a traditional hypervisor layer.\n\nThis shift allows teams running agentic code execution, developer workspaces, or complex multi-service containers to get deep isolation with near-container startup latencies. You can run system services and process managers cleanly while keeping host system calls mediated.\n\nEliminating virtual machine overhead for complex sandboxed workloads is a massive step forward for secure execution environments."
    },
    {
