      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50300003",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Small architectural oversights in autonomous agent runtimes can silently drain token budgets. Reverse engineering Claude Code reveals that a simple status line poll for background agents consumes 15 to 20 percent of overall token expenditures.\n\nEvery 30 seconds, the client forks a request containing the entire agent conversation transcript just to generate a 3-to-5 word status string. While modern prompt caching keeps warm requests cheap, long transcripts still incur heavy base overhead. Even worse, just two cache misses in a hundred requests account for nearly 45 percent of the status poll cost on large contexts.\n\nBecause the agent continuously appends new actions, cache invalidation occurs regularly. A harness designed to provide user feedback inadvertently creates an expensive distributed polling loop against the underlying foundation model.\n\nWhen designing agent frameworks, status tracking must be decoupled from full-context model invocations to prevent catastrophic cost scaling."
    },
    {
      "title": "System 1 models offer fast calibrated routine decisions beyond autoregression",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33775",
      "date": "2026-10-30",
      "authors": [
        "Haggai Roitman"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how single-forward-pass System 1 architectures provide cheaper, faster, and well-calibrated alternatives to autoregressive LLMs for routine decisions. It provides a concrete taxonomy and integration blueprint for building hybrid fast-slow agentic pipelines.",
      "hn_id": "50301027",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50301027",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Generating tokens one by one is an expensive way to make binary routing decisions or classify tool calls inside an agent loop. Many production pipelines still waste hundreds of milliseconds invoking full frontier models for tasks that require only a structured classification.\n\nAn emerging architectural pattern pairs fast non-autoregressive decision models with slow reasoning models into a cascaded hierarchy. These single-pass encoders and reward models act as calibrated gatekeepers, routing predictable queries instantly and escalating ambiguous edge cases to heavier reasoning systems.\n\nThe trickiest operational challenge is confidence calibration under distribution drift. Techniques like Reinforcement Learning for Calibrated Decisions provide reliable probabilistic thresholds, allowing teams to tune latency budgets without degrading downstream task accuracy.\n\nDecoupling routine classification from multi-step generation cuts latency and cost while keeping agent pipelines deterministic where it matters."
    },
    {
