      "content": "If you build Model Context Protocol tools for AI agents, having superior functionality does not guarantee the model will ever execute it. When the team behind IDA Pro launched an official MCP server, they discovered that coding agents consistently ignored it, falling back on basic utilities like objdump and readelf.\n\nThe culprit was not model reasoning capability or execution failure, but tool discovery. Different coding harnesses and CLI wrappers use wildly varying retrieval strategies to surface MCP tools into context windows, frequently truncating tool descriptions or relying on naive string matches.\n\nDesigning MCP endpoints requires treating your tool schemas as retrieval targets. Naming, description density, and argument annotations must be tuned for the specific search harness wrapping the agent. If the harness cannot index your tool effectively, your agent will never know it exists."
    },
    {
      "title": "Xazz unifies data preprocessing deep learning and static security guardrails",
