      "date": "2026-10-30",
      "authors": [
        "drdrey"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how machine-checked proofs can verify x86 binary execution end to end without trusting the compiler. You will learn how binary-level verification drastically shrinks the trusted computing base for critical software.",
      "hn_id": "50311319",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50311319",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Formal verification usually targets source code, which means your proofs depend on the assumption that compilers, linkers, and runtime optimizations introduce zero bugs. A new project from Zellic and V12 shifts the verification boundary down to the bare metal by producing machine-checked proofs directly on 64-bit x86 glibc binaries.\n\nVerifying at the binary level radically shrinks the trusted computing base. You no longer need to formalize complex compiler transformations or trust optimizer passes across different languages. However, modeling the x86 instruction set architecture is notoriously difficult due to dense legacy semantics, flag behaviors, and complex system call interfaces.\n\nThe authors combined frontier language models with expert human guidance to make the verification of full glibc binary executions scalable and reusable. This hybrid workflow offers a realistic path toward verifying security-critical binaries without requiring manual proof engineering for every single instruction.\n\nProving correctness at the binary level finally removes the compiler from your trusted computing base."
    },
    {
