      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/cole-h06/omneum",
      "date": "2026-10-30",
      "authors": [
        "cole-h06"
      ],
      "why_read": "Understand how to mitigate context rot in LLM agents through provenance-aware graph inference. You will learn how to optimize evidence selection under strict token budgets to maintain downstream accuracy.",
      "hn_id": "50304917",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50304917",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Longer context windows do not solve the problem of reasoning degradation in autonomous agents. As an agent gathers observations from tools, retrieval engines, and past execution sessions, noisy evidence accumulates and triggers context rot, degrading output accuracy even when the correct facts are present.\n\nOmneum addresses this challenge by functioning as an independent context control plane. Rather than passing raw text blobs into the prompt, the engine models evidence as a bipartite graph of sources and claims. It computes mutual reinforcement scores to evaluate source reliability alongside claim validity, filtering out low-utility tokens before they hit the model.\n\nOperating as a text-blind filter allows the system to enforce strict token budgets locally without running expensive extra inference passes over candidate documents. This approach mirrors classic link analysis algorithms like HITS, applying structured graph ranking to agent memory management.\n\nContext engineering is quickly becoming an algorithmic systems problem rather than a prompting trick."
