        "jonesd"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how triaging uncovered code lines by function exposes silently flawed tests in distributed systems. It offers a practical forensic approach to catch false confidence in test suites.",
      "hn_id": "50303146",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50303146",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Code coverage metrics often give distributed systems teams a false sense of security. Tracking overall percentages can easily hide tests that pass for the entirely wrong reasons, especially inside Byzantine fault-tolerant consensus state machines.\n\nIn a recent postmortem of a PBFT engine, an engineer noticed that a test called governance_receive_pre_prepare_validates was consistently green. The test delivered mutated proposals with wrong views and sequence numbers, asserting an error on every step. However, auditing dark lines revealed the validation logic never ran: an earlier, unrelated transport check was failing immediately and returning that error instead.\n\nTo catch these false positives, you should bucket uncovered branches by function rather than viewing them as a generic todo list. In critical distributed infrastructure, a passing test without explicit branch assertion is as dangerous as having no test at all.\n\nRelying on green tests without examining exact execution paths creates silent consensus bugs waiting to surface in production."
