      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how embedding a metric ingestion engine natively into PostgreSQL eliminates the complexity of external time-series databases. You will learn the mechanics behind achieving massive telemetry throughput using eBPF driver bypass and compact wire packing.",
      "hn_id": "50306146",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50306146",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Spinning up a separate time-series cluster like InfluxDB or Prometheus just to count events introduces unnecessary architectural complexity and maintenance overhead. Most teams accept this operational burden because relational databases historically buckle under write-heavy telemetry workloads.\n\nstat_pg challenges that assumption by embedding a StatsD-inspired ingestion engine directly inside PostgreSQL as a native background worker. Using eBPF XDP driver-level packet bypass, it ingests 9.5 million raw UDP packets per second while bypassing standard kernel network overhead entirely.\n\nThe engine relies on hierarchical cascading aggregation and columnar packing to compress updates down to 2.49 bytes per metric. This allows PostgreSQL to sustain over one billion metrics per second while keeping read latency below 0.11 milliseconds without lock contention.\n\nConsolidating your operational metrics into your primary database simplifies the stack without sacrificing throughput."
