      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50299811",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Designing algorithms under microsecond constraints forces delightful engineering trade-offs. The nibnib format is an image compression approach specifically engineered to fit within a strict budget of six clock cycles per pixel for DVI output on an RP2350 microcontroller.\n\nStandard palette lookups fall short in smooth color regions. Instead of storing absolute colors, nibnib stores palette delta values relative to the previous pixel. The decoder doubles the last decoded pixel and reads a single byte from the stream to look up a 32-bit delta, producing two 16-bit RGB 565 pixels in a single operation.\n\nThis yields a clean 4 bits-per-pixel compression scheme that decodes two pixels simultaneously with minimal arithmetic. When cycle budgets are counted on one hand, deterministic and branchless byte decoding beats complex entropy coding every time."
